WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:05.254 --> 00:00:07.744
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:07.746 --> 00:00:10.157
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:10.157 --> 00:00:12.470
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:12.472 --> 00:00:14.719
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:17.139
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:17.141 --> 00:00:19.361
things about the world at the same

00:00:19.361 --> 00:00:21.117
time as improving your English.

00:00:21.545 --> 00:00:24.753
I'm Alastair Budge, and today it's time

00:00:24.755 --> 00:00:27.878
for part three, the final part, of our

00:00:27.888 --> 00:00:30.837
three-part mini-series on dinosaurs.

00:00:31.265 --> 00:00:34.100
In case you missed them, in part one, we

00:00:34.102 --> 00:00:37.077
learned about the Bone Wars, the poisonous

00:00:37.116 --> 00:00:40.059
feud between two palaeontologists.

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:42.454
In part two, we followed the

00:00:42.456 --> 00:00:44.739
early science of dinosaurs, and

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:46.857
learned how it wasn't quite right.

00:00:47.534 --> 00:00:49.901
And today, in part three, we will

00:00:49.903 --> 00:00:52.196
end, fittingly enough, on almost

00:00:52.196 --> 00:00:55.456
certainly the worst day in the history

00:00:55.458 --> 00:00:58.201
of life on Earth, and undeniably

00:00:58.473 --> 00:01:00.878
the worst day for the dinosaurs.

00:01:01.313 --> 00:01:03.840
And that was sixty-six million

00:01:03.842 --> 00:01:06.535
years ago, the day it all ended.

00:01:06.898 --> 00:01:09.300
So, let's not waste a minute

00:01:09.302 --> 00:01:11.187
and get right into it.

00:01:12.542 --> 00:01:15.647
Every child, at least every budding

00:01:15.649 --> 00:01:18.840
young palaeontologist, asks the same

00:01:18.841 --> 00:01:21.523
question: what happened to the dinosaurs?

00:01:23.074 --> 00:01:25.964
For most of the twentieth century,

00:01:25.966 --> 00:01:28.855
science had no good answer at all.

00:01:29.932 --> 00:01:32.857
Scientists knew that sometime around

00:01:32.859 --> 00:01:36.269
sixty-six million years ago, the dinosaurs

00:01:36.324 --> 00:01:39.431
disappeared from the fossil record, along

00:01:39.433 --> 00:01:42.387
with a huge share of all life on Earth.

00:01:43.327 --> 00:01:46.163
The question was why, and the

00:01:46.165 --> 00:01:48.998
theories on offer ranged from

00:01:48.999 --> 00:01:51.858
the reasonable to the ridiculous.

00:01:52.701 --> 00:01:55.195
Perhaps the climate changed slowly,

00:01:55.197 --> 00:01:57.547
and the dinosaurs could not cope.

00:01:58.242 --> 00:02:01.412
Perhaps new kinds of plants poisoned

00:02:01.414 --> 00:02:04.229
them, or mammals ate their eggs.

00:02:05.083 --> 00:02:08.004
Perhaps, one theory seriously suggested,

00:02:08.006 --> 00:02:10.486
they had simply become too big and

00:02:10.486 --> 00:02:13.038
too stupid to survive, and died

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:15.837
of a kind of evolutionary old age.

00:02:16.689 --> 00:02:17.854
Nobody knew.

00:02:18.469 --> 00:02:20.899
The extinction was a mystery.

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:25.307
The process of solving it, interestingly

00:02:25.309 --> 00:02:27.744
enough, started with a man who was

00:02:27.747 --> 00:02:29.567
not looking for dinosaurs at all.

00:02:30.725 --> 00:02:34.401
In the 1970s, an American geologist

00:02:34.403 --> 00:02:37.761
named Walter Alvarez was working

00:02:37.772 --> 00:02:40.379
in the mountains of central Italy,

00:02:40.381 --> 00:02:42.910
near the town of Gubbio, studying

00:02:42.918 --> 00:02:45.189
the ancient layers of pink limestone

00:02:45.191 --> 00:02:47.270
that make up the hillsides there.

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:50.838
These rocks held a record of times

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:53.512
when the Earth's magnetic field had

00:02:53.513 --> 00:02:55.622
flipped, when a compass would have

00:02:55.624 --> 00:02:57.544
pointed south instead of north.

00:02:58.157 --> 00:03:00.130
That was what he was looking for.

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:03.536
But those same rocks held

00:03:03.538 --> 00:03:06.459
a record of something else.

00:03:07.751 --> 00:03:10.583
Running through the limestone

00:03:10.585 --> 00:03:13.513
was a thin layer of grey clay,

00:03:13.842 --> 00:03:15.468
about a centimetre thick.

00:03:16.823 --> 00:03:18.969
In the rock below the clay,

00:03:18.971 --> 00:03:20.956
there were fossils of all

00:03:20.958 --> 00:03:22.744
sorts of tiny sea creatures.

00:03:23.769 --> 00:03:26.249
In the rock just above it, most

00:03:26.251 --> 00:03:28.569
of those creatures were gone.

00:03:30.051 --> 00:03:32.912
The clay layer is the boundary:

00:03:32.914 --> 00:03:35.404
the exact geological moment

00:03:35.669 --> 00:03:37.850
when the old world ended.

00:03:39.127 --> 00:03:42.395
And it gave Alvarez a question he

00:03:42.397 --> 00:03:45.960
could investigate: how long had that

00:03:45.961 --> 00:03:49.244
centimetre of clay taken to build up?

00:03:49.874 --> 00:03:51.548
Was it a thousand years?

00:03:51.979 --> 00:03:52.519
A million?

00:03:52.953 --> 00:03:53.627
Ten million?

00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:56.839
The answer would tell him whether

00:03:56.841 --> 00:03:59.540
the extinction had been slow or fast.

00:04:01.011 --> 00:04:02.513
Fortunately, he had a

00:04:02.515 --> 00:04:04.372
father who could help him.

00:04:05.445 --> 00:04:08.016
Walter's father was Luis Alvarez,

00:04:08.018 --> 00:04:10.432
a Nobel Prize-winning physicist

00:04:10.712 --> 00:04:12.639
and one of the most inventive

00:04:12.641 --> 00:04:14.965
scientific minds of his generation.

00:04:16.058 --> 00:04:18.628
He had flown as a scientific observer

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:20.850
on the Hiroshima mission, helped

00:04:20.872 --> 00:04:23.996
invent radar navigation, and once

00:04:23.998 --> 00:04:27.498
used cosmic rays to X-ray an Egyptian

00:04:27.503 --> 00:04:29.986
pyramid, looking for hidden chambers.

00:04:30.947 --> 00:04:33.810
A sixty-six-million-year-old murder

00:04:33.812 --> 00:04:37.083
mystery was exactly his sort of problem.

00:04:37.857 --> 00:04:41.475
And together, the father and son team

00:04:41.477 --> 00:04:45.386
came up with an idea: measure the amount

00:04:45.541 --> 00:04:48.953
of an element called iridium in the clay.

00:04:50.257 --> 00:04:52.589
Iridium is a metal that is

00:04:52.591 --> 00:04:55.190
extremely rare in the Earth's

00:04:55.238 --> 00:04:58.622
crust, but common in meteorites.

00:04:59.825 --> 00:05:03.283
Some of it does drift down from space

00:05:03.285 --> 00:05:07.115
all the time, but at a slow, steady rate.

00:05:08.307 --> 00:05:11.375
Measure the iridium in a layer of rock,

00:05:11.377 --> 00:05:14.129
the Alvarezes reasoned, and you can

00:05:14.189 --> 00:05:18.354
estimate how long that layer took to form.

00:05:19.294 --> 00:05:21.490
It works a bit like the dust

00:05:21.492 --> 00:05:23.765
on a bookshelf: the more dust

00:05:23.779 --> 00:05:25.615
there is, the longer it has

00:05:25.617 --> 00:05:27.588
been since anyone cleaned it.

00:05:28.015 --> 00:05:31.541
So more iridium meant more time.

00:05:32.798 --> 00:05:34.922
They took samples of the Gubbio

00:05:34.924 --> 00:05:37.458
clay, and sent them off to be tested.

00:05:38.713 --> 00:05:40.624
When the results came back, it

00:05:40.626 --> 00:05:42.981
looked like there had been a mistake.

00:05:44.323 --> 00:05:47.595
The clay contained around thirty

00:05:47.597 --> 00:05:50.970
times more iridium than expected.

00:05:51.641 --> 00:05:54.965
If all of it had drifted down slowly from

00:05:54.967 --> 00:05:58.290
space, that thin layer of clay would have

00:05:58.306 --> 00:06:01.318
taken an impossibly long time to form.

00:06:02.637 --> 00:06:05.731
The iridium must have arrived

00:06:05.733 --> 00:06:09.145
some other way, and all at once.

00:06:10.445 --> 00:06:13.336
The same spike turned up in the boundary

00:06:13.338 --> 00:06:16.300
clay in Denmark, and then in New Zealand,

00:06:16.735 --> 00:06:18.952
and then at sites all around the world.

00:06:19.857 --> 00:06:22.300
Whatever had happened, it had

00:06:22.302 --> 00:06:24.659
happened everywhere at once.

00:06:25.933 --> 00:06:27.794
The Alvarezes followed the

00:06:27.796 --> 00:06:29.870
evidence to a conclusion that

00:06:29.870 --> 00:06:31.677
sounded like science fiction.

00:06:32.785 --> 00:06:35.645
In 1980, they published it.

00:06:36.649 --> 00:06:39.660
At the end of the Cretaceous, they

00:06:39.662 --> 00:06:42.582
argued, the Earth had been hit by

00:06:42.583 --> 00:06:45.576
an asteroid roughly ten kilometres

00:06:45.578 --> 00:06:48.833
across, a mountain of rock travelling

00:06:48.856 --> 00:06:51.614
at tens of kilometres per second.

00:06:52.624 --> 00:06:55.376
The impact had thrown up enough dust

00:06:55.378 --> 00:06:57.898
and debris into the atmosphere to

00:06:57.908 --> 00:07:01.293
darken the sky worldwide, shut down

00:07:01.295 --> 00:07:04.872
photosynthesis, collapse food chains,

00:07:05.171 --> 00:07:07.948
and kill off most of life on Earth,

00:07:07.950 --> 00:07:10.804
including, of course, the dinosaurs.

00:07:11.686 --> 00:07:15.247
The iridium layer was the asteroid

00:07:15.249 --> 00:07:18.179
itself, vaporised and spread

00:07:18.226 --> 00:07:20.822
thinly over the entire planet.

00:07:22.236 --> 00:07:25.242
The reaction from palaeontologists

00:07:25.244 --> 00:07:28.601
was, to put it mildly, unenthusiastic.

00:07:29.495 --> 00:07:32.470
Geology had spent a century and a half

00:07:32.472 --> 00:07:35.210
establishing that the Earth changes

00:07:35.241 --> 00:07:38.393
slowly, over millions of years, and that

00:07:38.395 --> 00:07:41.308
sudden catastrophes were the stuff of

00:07:41.382 --> 00:07:44.095
amateurs, cranks, and people interested

00:07:44.097 --> 00:07:46.878
in selling cheap science fiction novels.

00:07:48.212 --> 00:07:51.370
Now two outsiders, neither of them a

00:07:51.372 --> 00:07:54.440
dinosaur specialist, were proposing

00:07:54.450 --> 00:07:57.558
the biggest catastrophe imaginable.

00:07:58.868 --> 00:08:01.116
One palaeontologist dismissed

00:08:01.118 --> 00:08:03.520
the whole idea as "codswallop",

00:08:03.913 --> 00:08:06.181
which is an old-fashioned and very

00:08:06.183 --> 00:08:08.249
British way of saying nonsense.

00:08:09.355 --> 00:08:11.777
Even the New York Times published an

00:08:11.779 --> 00:08:14.200
editorial dismissing the whole idea.

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:17.054
Scientists, it said, “should leave

00:08:17.056 --> 00:08:19.416
astrologers the task of seeking the

00:08:19.426 --> 00:08:21.719
cause of earthly events in the stars.”

00:08:23.018 --> 00:08:25.854
And the sceptics had a fair

00:08:25.856 --> 00:08:29.426
question: if an asteroid that size

00:08:29.694 --> 00:08:32.032
hit the Earth, where is the hole?

00:08:33.187 --> 00:08:35.892
A ten-kilometre asteroid would leave a

00:08:35.894 --> 00:08:38.881
crater around two hundred kilometres wide.

00:08:39.637 --> 00:08:42.136
Craters that size do not simply

00:08:42.138 --> 00:08:44.796
disappear, and something that big

00:08:45.104 --> 00:08:46.664
should have been pretty obvious.

00:08:48.101 --> 00:08:51.059
For a decade, the missing crater was

00:08:51.061 --> 00:08:53.770
the great weakness in the theory.

00:08:54.853 --> 00:08:57.641
And without a crater, there were plenty

00:08:57.643 --> 00:09:00.001
of other competing theories about

00:09:00.030 --> 00:09:01.642
what had happened to the dinosaurs.

00:09:02.661 --> 00:09:05.161
The strongest pointed to India,

00:09:05.163 --> 00:09:07.823
where colossal volcanic eruptions

00:09:07.958 --> 00:09:10.558
were pouring out lava at just this

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:13.235
time, and argued that the dinosaurs

00:09:13.276 --> 00:09:16.027
had been slowly choked by volcanic

00:09:16.029 --> 00:09:18.697
gases, sort of poisoned to death.

00:09:19.822 --> 00:09:22.034
That debate, by the way, about how

00:09:22.036 --> 00:09:24.180
much the volcanoes contributed to

00:09:24.181 --> 00:09:26.427
the extinction, is still ongoing,

00:09:26.429 --> 00:09:28.810
although it is somewhat more niche.

00:09:30.083 --> 00:09:32.272
And the strange thing about the

00:09:32.274 --> 00:09:34.319
missing crater is that it had

00:09:34.379 --> 00:09:36.473
actually already been found.

00:09:37.756 --> 00:09:41.210
Back in the late 1970s, a geophysicist

00:09:41.212 --> 00:09:44.118
working for the Mexican national

00:09:44.136 --> 00:09:46.261
oil company had been surveying

00:09:46.263 --> 00:09:48.174
the Gulf of Mexico, looking

00:09:48.174 --> 00:09:50.062
for promising places to drill.

00:09:51.001 --> 00:09:54.110
His name was Glen Penfield, and on

00:09:54.112 --> 00:09:57.585
the northern coast of Mexico's Yucatán

00:09:57.597 --> 00:10:01.334
Peninsula, he found an enormous buried

00:10:01.336 --> 00:10:04.973
ring, half on land and half under the

00:10:04.974 --> 00:10:07.685
sea, about two hundred kilometres across.

00:10:08.919 --> 00:10:12.476
Penfield suspected an impact crater.

00:10:13.154 --> 00:10:15.492
But the data belonged to the

00:10:15.494 --> 00:10:17.913
oil company, and his findings

00:10:18.172 --> 00:10:20.675
ended up buried in company files.

00:10:21.823 --> 00:10:24.835
Only in 1991 did the scientific

00:10:24.837 --> 00:10:28.041
world formally connect the buried

00:10:28.041 --> 00:10:30.290
ring to the boundary layer.

00:10:31.224 --> 00:10:34.162
The crater was there, exactly the right

00:10:34.164 --> 00:10:37.024
size and exactly the right age, buried

00:10:37.239 --> 00:10:39.542
under a kilometre of younger rock.

00:10:40.548 --> 00:10:42.940
The dinosaurs had been killed

00:10:42.942 --> 00:10:45.331
by a massive rock from space.

00:10:46.304 --> 00:10:49.492
And now that scientists had broadly

00:10:49.494 --> 00:10:52.315
agreed on where and how hard it

00:10:52.324 --> 00:10:54.865
hit, they were able to reconstruct

00:10:54.867 --> 00:10:57.406
what the end actually looked like.

00:10:58.048 --> 00:11:00.542
So we are going to do that.

00:11:01.365 --> 00:11:03.898
Here is, as far as we know, the

00:11:03.900 --> 00:11:06.676
last day of the Cretaceous period.

00:11:08.008 --> 00:11:10.626
Sixty-six million years ago,

00:11:10.628 --> 00:11:13.618
North America is split in two by

00:11:13.625 --> 00:11:15.983
a seaway running down its middle.

00:11:16.842 --> 00:11:19.775
The place we now call Mexico is

00:11:19.777 --> 00:11:22.519
a warm, shallow tropical sea.

00:11:23.242 --> 00:11:25.966
The lands on either side are green:

00:11:25.968 --> 00:11:29.080
forests of conifers, ferns and flowering

00:11:29.085 --> 00:11:31.961
trees, riverbanks and floodplains.

00:11:33.112 --> 00:11:36.576
And the world is full of dinosaurs.

00:11:37.056 --> 00:11:39.158
Triceratops in herds.

00:11:39.462 --> 00:11:41.363
Duck-billed dinosaurs in their

00:11:41.365 --> 00:11:43.074
thousands along the rivers.

00:11:43.645 --> 00:11:45.619
Tyrannosaurus rex, the great

00:11:45.621 --> 00:11:47.945
predator of the age, near the top

00:11:47.957 --> 00:11:50.477
of a food web that has been more or

00:11:50.479 --> 00:11:52.925
less stable for millions of years.

00:11:54.130 --> 00:11:57.038
There is nothing unusual about this day.

00:11:57.652 --> 00:11:59.701
The animals feeding that morning are

00:11:59.703 --> 00:12:01.863
the descendants of a hundred and sixty

00:12:01.864 --> 00:12:04.935
million years of unbroken success.

00:12:06.090 --> 00:12:09.384
Then, in the sky over the Gulf,

00:12:09.386 --> 00:12:12.891
in a flash, there is a new light.

00:12:14.046 --> 00:12:15.634
The asteroid is about

00:12:15.636 --> 00:12:17.297
ten kilometres across.

00:12:18.344 --> 00:12:21.414
It's probably quite hard to picture that,

00:12:21.416 --> 00:12:24.558
but imagine Mount Everest, add a kilometre

00:12:24.558 --> 00:12:27.222
or so, and you are just about there.

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:31.678
It is travelling at something like twenty

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:34.795
kilometres per second, which is roughly a

00:12:34.856 --> 00:12:38.323
hundred times faster than a passenger jet.

00:12:39.402 --> 00:12:41.621
Even if one beady-eyed T.

00:12:41.622 --> 00:12:44.440
rex just so happened to be looking up

00:12:44.442 --> 00:12:47.410
into the sky at that very moment, right

00:12:47.434 --> 00:12:49.866
at the spot where the asteroid entered

00:12:49.868 --> 00:12:52.491
the Earth's atmosphere, it would all have

00:12:52.491 --> 00:12:55.291
been over before it had time to react.

00:12:56.575 --> 00:12:58.832
At that speed, the atmosphere

00:12:58.834 --> 00:13:00.699
might as well not exist.

00:13:01.871 --> 00:13:04.205
The asteroid passes through the

00:13:04.207 --> 00:13:06.916
entire thickness of the sky in a few

00:13:06.917 --> 00:13:10.146
seconds and strikes the shallow sea.

00:13:11.240 --> 00:13:13.895
The numbers involved stop meaning very

00:13:13.897 --> 00:13:16.341
much at this point, but let me try.

00:13:17.446 --> 00:13:19.992
The energy released is billions

00:13:19.994 --> 00:13:22.620
of times greater than the atomic

00:13:22.621 --> 00:13:24.639
bomb dropped on Hiroshima.

00:13:25.710 --> 00:13:28.386
The impact punches a hole in the

00:13:28.388 --> 00:13:31.230
Earth's crust ten kilometres deep.

00:13:32.407 --> 00:13:35.313
For a few minutes, the rock beneath

00:13:35.315 --> 00:13:38.218
the crater rebounds into a mountain

00:13:38.230 --> 00:13:40.574
higher than the Himalayas, before

00:13:40.576 --> 00:13:42.775
collapsing back into the Earth.

00:13:43.728 --> 00:13:46.906
Everything within about a thousand

00:13:46.908 --> 00:13:49.617
kilometres is simply gone, of

00:13:49.618 --> 00:13:51.747
course, including our beady-eyed T.

00:13:51.747 --> 00:13:53.277
rex and every other

00:13:53.279 --> 00:13:55.129
form of life around it.

00:13:56.298 --> 00:13:59.137
The seabed it strikes is rich in

00:13:59.139 --> 00:14:02.064
sulphur-bearing rock, and this is

00:14:02.064 --> 00:14:03.492
going to be important in a minute.

00:14:04.773 --> 00:14:07.734
Hundreds of billions of tonnes of rock,

00:14:07.736 --> 00:14:10.923
sulphur and sea water are blasted upwards,

00:14:11.247 --> 00:14:14.348
some of it clean out of the atmosphere.

00:14:15.544 --> 00:14:18.408
The Earth rings like a bell.

00:14:19.129 --> 00:14:21.582
The impact sets off earthquakes

00:14:21.584 --> 00:14:23.560
more powerful than any in

00:14:23.560 --> 00:14:25.089
recorded human history.

00:14:26.245 --> 00:14:28.732
If you are a Triceratops in what is

00:14:28.734 --> 00:14:31.433
now Montana, three thousand kilometres

00:14:31.448 --> 00:14:33.825
from the impact, you do not see the

00:14:33.827 --> 00:14:36.474
flash and you never hear the explosion.

00:14:37.211 --> 00:14:40.203
The first sign is the ground shaking.

00:14:41.485 --> 00:14:44.792
Around the Gulf, water surges into

00:14:44.794 --> 00:14:48.489
mega-tsunamis, waves perhaps a hundred

00:14:48.489 --> 00:14:51.156
metres or more high, which scour the

00:14:51.158 --> 00:14:53.600
coastlines of the young Atlantic.

00:14:54.751 --> 00:14:57.960
And then the sky begins to burn.

00:14:59.286 --> 00:15:01.525
All that rock blasted out of the

00:15:01.527 --> 00:15:03.554
crater has to come back down.

00:15:04.467 --> 00:15:07.147
Trillions of fragments, from dust

00:15:07.149 --> 00:15:09.746
grains to boulders, re-enter the

00:15:09.747 --> 00:15:12.114
atmosphere all over the world,

00:15:12.116 --> 00:15:14.560
each one glowing like a meteor.

00:15:16.028 --> 00:15:18.979
For an hour or so, the entire

00:15:18.981 --> 00:15:21.624
sky becomes an oven grill.

00:15:22.688 --> 00:15:25.970
In many places, just the heat is enough to

00:15:25.972 --> 00:15:29.251
kill animals below and set forests alight.

00:15:30.447 --> 00:15:32.858
Our Triceratops in Montana, if

00:15:32.860 --> 00:15:35.108
it has survived the shaking,

00:15:35.560 --> 00:15:36.874
does not survive this.

00:15:38.229 --> 00:15:41.259
And then comes a hard rain of tiny

00:15:41.261 --> 00:15:44.200
glass beads, falling like burning

00:15:44.201 --> 00:15:46.923
hail: the molten droplets of the

00:15:46.925 --> 00:15:49.475
vaporised asteroid and seabed.

00:15:50.867 --> 00:15:53.403
Terrible as it was, the first

00:15:53.405 --> 00:15:56.288
day was survivable, it's thought.

00:15:57.363 --> 00:16:00.339
Animals in burrows, animals in deep water,

00:16:00.341 --> 00:16:03.315
and animals on the far side of the planet,

00:16:03.703 --> 00:16:06.071
many of them would have made it through.

00:16:07.068 --> 00:16:09.366
For anything that survived the

00:16:09.368 --> 00:16:11.435
first day, however, another

00:16:11.471 --> 00:16:13.596
catastrophe was just beginning.

00:16:14.726 --> 00:16:16.880
It began with the sulphur in

00:16:16.882 --> 00:16:19.034
the rock at the impact site.

00:16:20.018 --> 00:16:22.851
Vast quantities of it were converted

00:16:22.853 --> 00:16:26.077
into a haze of fine droplets in the upper

00:16:26.101 --> 00:16:28.686
atmosphere, joining the soot from the

00:16:28.688 --> 00:16:31.341
fires and the finest of the rock dust.

00:16:32.345 --> 00:16:35.118
That haze spread around the entire

00:16:35.120 --> 00:16:37.401
planet, and it stayed there.

00:16:38.699 --> 00:16:42.054
And beneath it, the lights went out.

00:16:43.055 --> 00:16:46.543
For years, sunlight at the surface was

00:16:46.545 --> 00:16:49.939
reduced to a fraction of normal, like

00:16:49.940 --> 00:16:53.661
a deep Swedish winter, but everywhere.

00:16:54.657 --> 00:16:57.996
Global temperatures fell hard; in the

00:16:57.998 --> 00:17:01.515
oceans and on land, the world entered a

00:17:01.527 --> 00:17:05.122
sudden, deep winter that lasted years.

00:17:06.631 --> 00:17:10.648
On the ground, noon was as dim as dusk.

00:17:11.338 --> 00:17:14.749
There were no seasons any more, only cold.

00:17:15.631 --> 00:17:17.421
The forests were standing

00:17:17.423 --> 00:17:19.497
skeletons, because the leaves

00:17:19.498 --> 00:17:21.555
that fell were never replaced.

00:17:22.687 --> 00:17:25.775
Rivers still ran, and dead things

00:17:25.777 --> 00:17:29.331
still rotted, but almost nothing grew.

00:17:30.094 --> 00:17:32.475
And it stayed like this, month

00:17:32.477 --> 00:17:34.776
after month, year after year.

00:17:36.097 --> 00:17:38.286
Photosynthesis, the process

00:17:38.288 --> 00:17:40.638
by which plants turn sunlight

00:17:40.659 --> 00:17:43.074
into food, slowed towards a

00:17:43.076 --> 00:17:46.205
standstill, on land and in the sea.

00:17:47.524 --> 00:17:50.982
And when the plants stop, everything above

00:17:50.984 --> 00:17:54.275
them in the food chain starts to starve,

00:17:54.692 --> 00:17:57.939
in strict order of size and appetite.

00:17:59.151 --> 00:18:01.471
A Triceratops needed to eat perhaps

00:18:01.473 --> 00:18:03.525
a hundred kilograms of plants a

00:18:03.526 --> 00:18:06.171
day, and there were herds of them.

00:18:06.953 --> 00:18:08.687
A Tyrannosaurus needed

00:18:08.689 --> 00:18:10.342
herds of Triceratops.

00:18:11.619 --> 00:18:14.912
Cut off the sunlight for a few years, and

00:18:14.914 --> 00:18:18.205
that whole towering structure comes down.

00:18:19.371 --> 00:18:21.677
By the time the skies cleared,

00:18:21.679 --> 00:18:24.136
something like three quarters of

00:18:24.220 --> 00:18:27.134
all species on Earth had gone.

00:18:27.655 --> 00:18:29.841
Every dinosaur, except the

00:18:29.843 --> 00:18:32.447
birds, was dead: every species,

00:18:32.850 --> 00:18:34.268
everywhere on the planet.

00:18:35.080 --> 00:18:36.900
The pterosaurs, the great

00:18:36.902 --> 00:18:38.866
flying reptiles, were gone.

00:18:39.207 --> 00:18:42.108
The huge marine reptiles were gone too.

00:18:42.526 --> 00:18:45.296
The ammonites, the coiled shellfish that

00:18:45.298 --> 00:18:47.996
had filled the oceans for three hundred

00:18:48.006 --> 00:18:50.780
million years, they too were gone.

00:18:51.960 --> 00:18:55.549
So who survived, and why?

00:18:56.818 --> 00:18:58.814
On land, almost nothing much

00:18:58.816 --> 00:19:01.237
bigger than a cat made it through.

00:19:02.037 --> 00:19:03.762
The animals that survived

00:19:03.764 --> 00:19:05.902
tended to be small, which meant

00:19:06.149 --> 00:19:07.775
not needing very much food.

00:19:08.986 --> 00:19:12.005
Many were burrowers, living underground.

00:19:12.974 --> 00:19:15.365
A burrow, of course, provides

00:19:15.367 --> 00:19:18.004
shelter from both heat and cold.

00:19:19.143 --> 00:19:22.106
Freshwater creatures also did strangely

00:19:22.108 --> 00:19:24.918
well, because rivers and ponds had an

00:19:24.919 --> 00:19:27.694
abundance of dead leaves and rotting

00:19:27.696 --> 00:19:30.237
material: a food supply, in other

00:19:30.238 --> 00:19:32.838
words, that does not depend on sunshine.

00:19:34.037 --> 00:19:36.013
Crocodiles made it, being

00:19:36.015 --> 00:19:38.226
cold-blooded scavengers that

00:19:38.226 --> 00:19:40.139
can go months without a meal.

00:19:40.780 --> 00:19:41.741
Turtles made it.

00:19:42.136 --> 00:19:44.555
Small mammals, seed-eaters,

00:19:44.557 --> 00:19:47.871
insect-eaters, they also made it, and

00:19:48.090 --> 00:19:50.437
our own ancestors were among them.

00:19:51.708 --> 00:19:54.970
And some birds made it, just barely.

00:19:55.883 --> 00:19:58.615
The birds that survived seem to have

00:19:58.617 --> 00:20:01.347
been small ground-dwellers, and they

00:20:01.347 --> 00:20:04.337
had one priceless piece of equipment:

00:20:04.339 --> 00:20:07.247
a toothless beak, which was good for

00:20:07.314 --> 00:20:09.820
picking seeds out of the ruined ground.

00:20:11.179 --> 00:20:14.408
From those few survivors came every

00:20:14.410 --> 00:20:17.544
bird alive today, all ten thousand

00:20:17.544 --> 00:20:20.878
species, from hummingbirds to ostriches.

00:20:22.299 --> 00:20:26.167
And slowly, things started to get better.

00:20:27.262 --> 00:20:28.858
You can see it in the rocks.

00:20:29.419 --> 00:20:31.288
In the layers just above the

00:20:31.290 --> 00:20:33.090
boundary, there is a sudden

00:20:33.131 --> 00:20:36.127
explosion of fern spores.

00:20:36.926 --> 00:20:40.133
Ferns are the first green things back

00:20:40.135 --> 00:20:43.340
after a landslide or a lava flow, and

00:20:43.664 --> 00:20:46.990
for a while, much of the land was a world

00:20:46.992 --> 00:20:50.234
of ferns, growing over the wreckage like

00:20:50.246 --> 00:20:53.245
weeds through the ruins of a bombed city.

00:20:54.516 --> 00:20:57.578
For the mammals, and that includes you and

00:20:57.580 --> 00:21:00.567
me, the catastrophe was the making of us.

00:21:01.412 --> 00:21:04.138
For a hundred and sixty million years,

00:21:04.140 --> 00:21:06.863
in the interests of self-preservation,

00:21:07.306 --> 00:21:10.628
mammals had lived small, mostly nocturnal

00:21:10.630 --> 00:21:13.625
lives in the shadow of the dinosaurs.

00:21:14.841 --> 00:21:17.440
Within a few hundred thousand years of

00:21:17.442 --> 00:21:20.176
the impact, they were growing, spreading

00:21:20.441 --> 00:21:23.304
and diversifying into the empty world.

00:21:24.248 --> 00:21:27.350
Within ten million years or so, some of

00:21:27.352 --> 00:21:30.691
them had taken to the trees, and developed

00:21:30.704 --> 00:21:33.745
grasping hands and forward-facing eyes.

00:21:34.625 --> 00:21:36.621
That long evolutionary

00:21:36.623 --> 00:21:39.070
road, of course, led to us.

00:21:40.335 --> 00:21:43.373
And the strangest fact about

00:21:43.375 --> 00:21:46.737
this last day is this: it could

00:21:46.738 --> 00:21:48.915
very easily not have happened.

00:21:49.910 --> 00:21:52.126
The extreme severity of the

00:21:52.128 --> 00:21:54.834
extinction seems to have depended

00:21:54.852 --> 00:21:57.899
on where, exactly, the asteroid hit.

00:21:59.182 --> 00:22:01.908
The shallow sea over the Yucatán was

00:22:01.910 --> 00:22:04.937
rich in sulphur-bearing rock, and it was

00:22:04.941 --> 00:22:07.412
sulphur, perhaps more than anything else,

00:22:07.414 --> 00:22:09.944
that turned what would otherwise have been

00:22:10.231 --> 00:22:13.881
a regional disaster into a global winter.

00:22:15.109 --> 00:22:17.822
Some researchers have estimated that only

00:22:17.824 --> 00:22:20.402
a small fraction of the Earth's surface

00:22:20.762 --> 00:22:23.334
could have produced an effect so severe.

00:22:24.292 --> 00:22:26.430
The Earth is constantly turning.

00:22:26.872 --> 00:22:29.453
If the asteroid had arrived a few

00:22:29.455 --> 00:22:31.955
hours earlier or later, it would

00:22:31.977 --> 00:22:34.804
almost certainly have struck deep

00:22:34.806 --> 00:22:37.460
ocean, and much of that sulphur

00:22:37.872 --> 00:22:39.433
would never have reached the sky.

00:22:40.308 --> 00:22:42.228
A terrible day, certainly.

00:22:42.285 --> 00:22:44.593
But perhaps not the end of the world.

00:22:45.691 --> 00:22:48.212
In that slightly different world,

00:22:48.214 --> 00:22:50.581
there is no reason to think the

00:22:50.585 --> 00:22:52.465
dinosaurs would have gone anywhere.

00:22:53.467 --> 00:22:55.587
They had already ruled for a hundred

00:22:55.589 --> 00:22:57.825
and sixty million years, through every

00:22:57.828 --> 00:22:59.905
change the planet had thrown at them.

00:23:00.706 --> 00:23:04.059
And if the dinosaurs had carried on, it's

00:23:04.061 --> 00:23:07.166
very hard to see how a clever, upright

00:23:07.230 --> 00:23:09.965
ape would ever have been given its chance.

00:23:10.969 --> 00:23:13.798
A few hours of planetary rotation,

00:23:13.800 --> 00:23:16.127
in other words, separate the

00:23:16.136 --> 00:23:18.011
dinosaurs' world from ours.

00:23:19.361 --> 00:23:22.612
Sixty-six million years later, a curious

00:23:22.614 --> 00:23:26.026
species of mammal would dig the old rulers

00:23:26.070 --> 00:23:28.853
out of the ground, fight over them, debate

00:23:28.855 --> 00:23:31.504
how to put them together again, get them

00:23:31.506 --> 00:23:34.851
wrong, get them right, and finally piece

00:23:34.853 --> 00:23:37.945
together the story of their last day.

00:23:40.061 --> 00:23:42.305
OK, then, that is it for today's

00:23:42.307 --> 00:23:44.479
episode, and for our three-part

00:23:44.481 --> 00:23:46.263
mini-series on dinosaurs.

00:23:46.495 --> 00:23:48.260
I hope it's been an interesting one

00:23:48.262 --> 00:23:50.125
and that you've learnt something new.

00:23:50.527 --> 00:23:52.633
If you missed the earlier parts, in

00:23:52.635 --> 00:23:54.678
part one we covered the Bone Wars,

00:23:54.714 --> 00:23:57.095
and in part two, the long argument

00:23:57.097 --> 00:23:59.756
about what dinosaurs were really like.

00:24:00.492 --> 00:24:02.921
You've been listening to English Learning

00:24:02.923 --> 00:24:05.172
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:24:05.569 --> 00:24:08.420
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:24:08.422 --> 00:24:11.045
I'll catch you in the next episode.
